   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello Phil<div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">I am trying\r\nto fit a bifactor model with only one specific factor. There are 12 items in\r\nthe test. Seven items (in random order) load on a specific factor but the other\r\n5 items don’t load on any specific factor and load only on the general factor. How\r\nshould I specify this in ‘mirt’?</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">If there\r\nwere two specific factors, that would have been easy like below. But with only\r\none specific factor I don&#39;t know what to do. What should I insert for the items that only belong to the general factor? </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:rgb(0,112,192)\">specific &lt;- c</span><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%;color:rgb(0,112,192)\">(1,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,2,1,2,2)</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> Thanks for your help.</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Afshin </span></p><div><br></div><span class=\"gmail_signature_prefix\">-- </span><br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><br><div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "p7gN5ggfBAAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2023-05-09T00:38:02Z",
   "subject": "Re: Bifactor model with only 1 specific factor",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">You can use NA placeholders to indicate that only the general factor loads in an item. So something like <div><br></div><div>specific &lt;- c(1,1,NA,1,1,1,NA,1,NA,1,NA,NA)</div><div><br></div><div>would work in your case.</div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
